"# aos-fsm: one step of a state table\n\n```json\n{\"_entry\": [\"aos-fsm\", {\"$env\": \"AOS_CF\"}], \"start\": \"a\", \"states\": {\n \"a\": {\"exec\": {\"argv\": [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"echo in a\"]}, \"next\": \"b\"},\n \"b\": {\"exec\": {\"argv\": [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"echo in b; exit 3\"]}, \"next\": {\"3\": \"a\", \"*\": \"b\"}}}}\n```\n\n```sh\nAOS_STACK=/tmp/st aos-fsm fsm.json      # prints \"in a\"; /tmp/st/stage.txt is now b\nAOS_STACK=/tmp/st aos-loop --max 3 fl.json   # fl.json: {\"exec\": \"fsm.json\"}: in a, in b, in a\n```\n\nThe current state is in `$AOS_STACK/stage.txt` (none = `start`). `next` as a name = move only on exit 0; as an object = look up the exit code (`*` = any). Moved = exit 0. A code the table does not catch = the step's own code, state unchanged. No `AOS_STACK` = `StackMissing`. The `_entry` key lets aos-exec and aos-loop run the table. An agent-fsm's `fsm.json` is such a table (idle, think, wait, act).\n"
